---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import BuildUpLink from "../../components/UnderTheHood/BuildUpLink.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
import Basics from "../../component-examples/menu/Basics.astro"
---

<TechniquePost slug="menu-popover-anchor">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      A dropdown menu used to be all JavaScript: toggle it, close it on
      {" "}<kbd>Esc</kbd> and on a click outside, fight <code>z-index</code> and
      {" "}<code>overflow: hidden</code> ancestors, and run a positioning library
      that measures the trigger and flips the menu near the viewport edge.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>popover</code> puts the menu in the top layer, with light dismiss and
        {" "}<kbd>Esc</kbd>
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>commandfor</code> and
        {" "}<code>command=&quot;toggle-popover&quot;</code> open it from a button
      </li>
      <li>
        The invoker is the implicit anchor, so there is no
        {" "}<code>anchor-name</code> to wire up
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>position-area</code> places it below the trigger, and
        {" "}<code>anchor-size(inline)</code> keeps it at least as wide. The <code
          >self-</code
        > keywords read the menu's own writing mode and direction, not the containing
        block's
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>max-block-size</code> with <code>100%</code> caps it to the space on
        its side, where it scrolls. <code>calc-size()</code> keeps at least <code
          >12rem</code
        > of it, or 40% of a short viewport, so it only flips when that side has less
        room
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>position-try-fallbacks</code> flips it when it would overflow. Two
        {" "}<code>@position-try</code> rules move it below or above, spanning both
        sides, when neither side fits
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`.menu[popover] {
  inset: auto;
  margin: 0.25rem 0;
  min-inline-size: max(12rem, anchor-size(inline));
  position-area: self-block-end span-self-inline-end;
  position-try-fallbacks:
    flip-block,
    flip-inline,
    flip-block flip-inline,
    --menu-block-end,
    --menu-block-start;
}

@position-try --menu-block-end {
  margin: 0.25rem 0;
  position-area: self-block-end span-all;
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Open the menu, then scroll until the button is near an edge and open it
      again.
    </p>
    <Example centered row showCode={false}>
      <Basics />
    </Example>
    <BuildUpLink />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Without anchor positioning the popover still opens, light dismisses and
        works with the keyboard, but it isn't placed next to its trigger
      </li>
      <li>
        Without Invoker Commands the button does nothing.
        {" "}<code>popovertarget</code> or <code>showPopover()</code> are the older
        ways in
      </li>
      <li>
        On iOS, submenus anchor to their parent menu instead of their trigger
        item: an <code>@supports (-webkit-touch-callout: none)</code> block in the
        compat layer (<code>css/compat/menu.css</code>) sets <code
          >anchor-name</code
        > on every menu that has a submenu, and <code>anchor-scope</code> only on
        the root menu, so a submenu three levels deep still finds its own parent
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
